[llm-docs
  [doc [#
docs-src/llm/manifest.cxd — the LLM onboarding layer's document set (#938).

Read by scripts/gen_docs/primer_build.cx (`make docs`). Declares:

  [site]     the published base URL every llms.txt link is written against
  [tagline]  the one-line `>` blurb at the top of llms.txt
  [suites]   the conformance suites the templates may cite, one per line,
             relative to conformance/ and without the .cxd extension.
             Declaring them bounds the fixture index (code.cxd alone is
             ~1200 cases) and records the primer's provenance in one place.
  [doc]      this block — ignored by the generator.
  [output]   one output file per entry, in llms-full.txt concatenation
             order. `section=` files the entry under one of llms.txt's
             headings — `start` | `build` | `reference` (default
             `reference`). The vocabulary is CLOSED: an unknown `section=`
             is a hard failure, because llms.txt IS the discovery surface
             and a document missing from it is a document nobody loads.

Adding a section that cites a new suite = add the suite here. A template
citing an undeclared (or nonexistent) fixture id is a HARD generator
failure, never a silently empty block.

RETIRING a document means deleting its file in the SAME change that drops
its [output] entry: `--check` fails on any file under docs/llm/ that no
entry claims (an ORPHAN), because a file the manifest does not know about
is one the freshness contract cannot reach.
#]]

  [; `base` addresses the reference set; `root` is the SITE ROOT, where the
     two llmstxt.org files live (the convention is /llms.txt — a copy under
     /llm/ is not discoverable and does not count). scripts/publish.sh puts
     them in both places and asserts the root pair arrived. ]
  [site base='https://cxhome.org/llm/' root='https://cxhome.org/']

  [tagline [#
CX is a homoiconic data-and-code language: one bracketed element syntax for
documents, queries, programs and its own AST. It post-dates every model's
training data, so this layer is the context an assistant needs to write it
correctly.
#]]

  [suites [#
code
core
llm/antipatterns
operator_heads
stdlib/store
stdlib/journal
stdlib/http
stdlib/mcp
stdlib/a2a
stdlib/strings
stdlib/fp
stdlib/json
stdlib/map
stdlib/array
stdlib/re
stdlib/time
stdlib/io
stdlib/env
stdlib/cx
stdlib/format
stdlib/authz
stdlib/session
stdlib/log
stdlib/ux
stdlib/ux-web
stdlib/xap-compose
stdlib/xap-dist
xml
table
identity
#]]

  [output id=primer out='primer.md' template='primer.md.tmpl'
          section=start
          title='CX primer for LLM assistants'
    [summary [#
The one file to load before writing CX. The surface taught through runnable
examples, a ring decision table, the core idioms, and the anti-patterns that
Lisp/Clojure/shell priors produce — each with its real diagnostic.
#]]]

  [output id=data out='reference-data-language.md'
          template='reference-data-language.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the CX data language'
    [summary [#
Elements, attributes, scalars and their auto-typing, collections, raw text,
tables, identity anchors, and the format conversions the data reading offers.
#]]]

  [output id=code out='reference-code-language.md'
          template='reference-code-language.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the CX code language'
    [summary [#
The directive surface — binding, control flow, comprehensions, pattern
matching, definitions, pipes, CXPath — and how errors propagate as values.
#]]]

  [output id=stdlib out='reference-stdlib.md'
          template='reference-stdlib.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the standard library'
    [summary [#
The bundled module catalog, the [?lib] import form, and the capability each
effectful pack requires.
#]]]

  [output id=cli out='reference-cli.md' template='reference-cli.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the cx command line'
    [summary [#
The run surface, the convert surface, the subcommand catalog, and the
capability grant flags — including the flag-placement rule that silently
denies capabilities when broken.
#]]]

  [output id=playbook-xap out='playbook-xap.md'
          template='playbook-xap.md.tmpl'
          section=build
          title='Playbook: building a production XAP'
    [summary [#
The full arc for a production feature deployment: authoring and composing
feature grammars, derived nouns and deriver principals, the authority model,
the *.xap.cxd deployment document and its [runtime] bindings, offline identity
bootstrap, the deployment host, and the ux-web hypermedia surface — with what
the rulings forbid stated as plainly as what they require.
#]]]

  [output id=platform out='reference-platform.md'
          template='reference-platform.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the platform and ecosystem rings'
    [summary [#
Ring 2 (store, journal, HTTP, XAP host) and Ring 3 (agent interop, feature
distribution, language bindings) — what is reachable and how.
#]]]
]
